"fetch_on_demand\":[\"tooling-proselint\",\"tooling-pre-commit\",\"audience-architects\",\"tooling-overview\"]}],\"warnings\":[],\"gap\":null}", "tool_name": null, "tool_input": null, "tool_summary": null, "is_error": false, "tokens": null, "subtype": null, "summary": null, "model": null, "tools": null, "mcp_servers": null, "duration_ms": null, "status": null, "native": {"id": "prt_f931242490018nEM6IBc1kFkvA", "sessionID": "ses_06ceddc21ffejHkDdX36qowp1b", "messageID": "msg_f93122873001KtA51fFtzfcmBs", "type": "tool", "callID": "call_jILYvh5TOSMlbzOpjbGlbJ29", "tool": "quartermaster_resolve_kits", "state": {"status": "completed", "input": {"task": "Inspect this repository''s CLI structure and refine a GitHub issue for engineering by identifying only the unresolved technical questions needed to implement a simple hello-world CLI command.", "limit": 4, "max_sections_per_kit": 4}, "output": "{\"engine\":\"embedding\",\"inferred_traits\":{\"languages\":[\"markdown\",\"shell\",\"yaml\",\"python\"],\"frameworks\":[\"mkdocs\",\"sphinx\",\"openshift\",\"docker\"],\"capabilities\":[\"merge-request-review\",\"github flow\",\"collaboration\",\"merge request\"],\"contexts\":[\"cli\",\"library\",\"automation\",\"ci\"],\"provenance\":[{\"category\":\"languages\",\"value\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"languages\",\"value\":\"shell\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"languages\",\"value\":\"yaml\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"languages\",\"value\":\"python\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"frameworks\",\"value\":\"mkdocs\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"frameworks\",\"value\":\"sphinx\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"frameworks\",\"value\":\"openshift\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"frameworks\",\"value\":\"docker\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"capabilities\",\"value\":\"merge-request-review\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"capabilities\",\"value\":\"github flow\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"capabilities\",\"value\":\"collaboration\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"capabilities\",\"value\":\"merge request\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"contexts\",\"value\":\"cli\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"contexts\",\"value\":\"library\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"contexts\",\"value\":\"automation\",\"source\":\"embedding\"},{\"category\":\"contexts\",\"value\":\"ci\",\"source\":\"embedding\"}]},\"confidence\":\"high\",\"coverage\":0.75,\"broadening_recommended\":false,\"kits\":[{\"name\":\"git-and-collaboration\",\"version\":\"v1\",\"score\":136,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reasons\":[\"match:languages\",\"match:capabilities\",\"match:contexts\",\"require-ok:capabilities\"],\"summary\":\"Git, GitLab, DTN release-governance defaults, and collaboration guidance.\",\"sections\":[{\"id\":\"invariant\",\"title\":\"Invariants\",\"gloss\":\"Use git, store code in GitLab, follow DTN release governance, and allow only narrow GitHub Flow exceptions.\",\"relevance\":0.0,\"always_load\":true},{\"id\":\"collaboration-modes\",\"title\":\"Collaboration Modes\",\"gloss\":\"Tradeoffs among merge requests, shared access, and hybrid workflows.\",\"relevance\":0.552,\"always_load\":false},{\"id\":\"visibility\",\"title\":\"Visibility\",\"gloss\":\"When to use internal visibility and when to restrict to private.\",\"relevance\":0.466,\"always_load\":false}],\"always_load_markdown\":\"# Invariants\\n\\n- Use `git` as the version control system.\\n- Store source code in GitLab.\\n- DTN projects follow `release-governance-dtn` for release branching and\\n  approval flow.\\n- A lighter GitHub Flow model is allowed only for small solo-dev\\n  projects with explicit COSS agreement.\\n- Treat security-related ignores and exceptions as reviewed changes, not local\\n  shortcuts.\\n\",\"fetch_on_demand\":[\"collaboration-modes\",\"visibility\"]},{\"name\":\"python-project\",\"version\":\"v1\",\"score\":134,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reasons\":[\"match:languages\",\"match:contexts\",\"require-ok:languages\"],\"summary\":\"Python project defaults for layout, formatting, lint, and security guidance.\",\"sections\":[{\"id\":\"invariant\",\"title\":\"Invariants\",\"gloss\":\"Python repos need pyproject-based tooling and defer source-style rules to the Python style kit.\",\"relevance\":0.0,\"always_load\":true},{\"id\":\"tooling\",\"title\":\"Tooling\",\"gloss\":\"Recommended linting and security tooling for Python projects.\",\"relevance\":0.602,\"always_load\":false}],\"always_load_markdown\":\"# Invariants\\n\\n- Python projects should use `pyproject.toml` as the main project metadata and\\n  tool configuration file.\\n- Use `black` with line length `80`.\\n- Use `isort` with the `black` profile.\\n- Application repositories should include `requirements.txt` with pinned\\n  dependencies.\\n- Python docstring and source-style rules follow\\n  `module-code-style-python`.\\n- Follow the cross-language formatting and repo layout kits unless Python needs\\n  a narrower rule.\\n\",\"fetch_on_demand\":[\"tooling\"]},{\"name\":\"module-code-style-python\",\"version\":\"v1\",\"score\":110,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"reasons\":[\"match:languages\",\"require-ok:languages\"],\"summary\":\"Source-of-truth Python source-style and docstring conventions.\",\"sections\":[{\"id\":\"prohibited-patterns\",\"title\":\"Prohibited patterns\",\"gloss\":\"Docstring and import anti-patterns that are forbidden, such as duplicated type info.\",\"relevance\":0.0,\"always_load\":true},{\"id\":\"overview\",\"title\":\"Overview\",\"gloss\":\"What this kit covers and when to load it for Python backend projects.\",\"relevance\":0.642,\"always_load\":false},{\"id\":\"docstring-requirements\",\"title\":\"Docstring requirements\",\"gloss\":\"Source-of-truth Python docstring rules: Sphinx style, public-symbol coverage, and consistent formatting.\",\"relevance\":0.595,\"always_load\":false},{\"id\":\"type-hints\",\"title\":\"Type hints\",\"gloss\":\"Require full annotations on every function, future annotations, and avoiding bare Any.\",\"relevance\":0.574,\"always_load\":false},{\"id\":\"import-rules\",\"title\":\"Import rules\",\"gloss\":\"Keep imports at module top level; use importlib plus Protocol for dynamic lookups.\",\"relevance\":0.528,\"always_load\":false}],\"always_load_markdown\":\"## Prohibited patterns\\n\\n- Docstrings with hard-coded type information that duplicates type\\n  annotations (`:type param:` style) \u2014 use annotations only.\\n- Missing module-level docstrings on any public Python file.\\n- Docstrings that merely restate the function name without adding\\n  useful information.\\n- Import statements buried in function bodies to work around runtime\\n  import errors (use `importlib` + `Protocol` instead).\\n\",\"fetch_on_demand\":[\"overview\",\"docstring-requirements\",\"type-hints\",\"import-rules\"]},{\"name\":\"module-documentation\",\"version\":\"v1\",\"score\":62,\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"reasons\":[],\"summary\":\"Source-of-truth documentation authoring rules: line length, format conventions, audience guidance, and a doc-quality tool stack.\",\"sections\":[{\"id\":\"universal-rules\",\"title\":\"Universal rules\",\"gloss\":\"Always-valid core: 80-char lines, semantic line breaks, audience-first writing, plain style.\",\"relevance\":0.0,\"always_load\":true},{\"id\":\"tooling-proselint\",\"title\":\"Tooling: proselint\",\"gloss\":\"Prose linting: when to enable it, config, and the terse-imperative false-positive caveat.\",\"relevance\":0.605,\"always_load\":false},{\"id\":\"tooling-pre-commit\",\"title\":\"Tooling: pre-commit\",\"gloss\":\"Wiring cspell, proselint, and a line-length check into a local shift-left commit gate.\",\"relevance\":0.605,\"always_load\":false},{\"id\":\"audience-architects\",\"title\":\"Audience: architects\",\"gloss\":\"Architecture docs: system context, decisions and rationale, trade-offs, supporting diagrams.\",\"relevance\":0.59,\"always_load\":false},{\"id\":\"tooling-overview\",\"title\":\"Tooling: overview and ROI\",\"gloss\":\"The doc-quality stack and ROI verdicts, including why redpen is excluded and proselint is opt-in.\",\"relevance\":0.577,\"always_load\":false}],\"always_load_markdown\":\"## Universal rules\\n\\nThese apply to every documentation file regardless of format or\\naudience.\\n\\n- **Wrap prose at 80 characters.** Hard-wrap text lines so no line\\n  exceeds 80 columns. This keeps diffs reviewable and docs readable in\\n  any terminal, editor, or side-by-side view.\\n- **Break lines on meaning, not just width.** Prefer semantic line\\n  breaks: end a line at a clause or sentence boundary when it falls\\n  near the margin. One sentence per line is also fine and produces the\\n  cleanest diffs. Never reflow a whole paragraph for a one-word edit.\\n- **Exceptions to the 80-char limit** are narrow and per-format: long\\n  URLs, table rows, code samples, and paths that cannot break. See the\\n  relevant `format-*` section. Do not contort content to fit \u2014 break\\n  the *line*, not the *meaning*.\\n- **Identify the audience before writing.** End users, developers,\\n  architects, operators, and reference readers need different depth and\\n  tone. Pick the matching `audience-*` section and write to it.\\n- **Keep audience boundaries intact.** Do not pull implementation\\n  detail into user-facing docs unless the reader must act on it.\\n  Framework, authentication, and platform choices usually belong in\\n  developer or maintainer documentation, not end-user pages.\\n- **Control duplication within one audience scope.** Prefer one\\n  canonical owner for each concept within a single audience scope.\\n  Within that scope, repeated material should usually become a precise\\n  cross-link, a short summary sentence, or a narrower page boundary.\\n- **Allow same-scope repetition only when it adds local value.** Repeat\\n  content in the same audience scope only when removing it would\\n  materially harm local comprehension, lookup speed, or task\\n  completion. Do not duplicate text just because pasting is easier than\\n  linking.\\n- **Allow cross-audience restatement when it is adapted.** The same\\n  fact may appear in end-user, developer, operator, or architect docs\\n  when each version is rewritten for that audience''s goal,\\n  vocabulary, and decisions under pressure.\\n- **Ask three questions before repeating content.** Who owns this\\n  concept? Is this repetition cross-audience or same-audience? Does\\n  this repetition add real local value here?\\n- **Write plainly.** Prefer active voice, short sentences, and concrete\\n  words. Cut filler (\\\"in order to\\\" \u2192 \\\"to\\\", \\\"utilize\\\" \u2192 \\\"use\\\"). Lead\\n  with the reader''s goal, not the system''s internals.\\n- **Use native semantic markup when it fits.** Prefer the documentation\\n  framework''s built-in semantic constructs when they match the content:\\n  notes, warnings, navigation blocks, procedures, reference fields,\\n  tables, and similar structures. Do not reduce every document to plain\\n  headings and bullet lists when the format has a clearer native form.\\n- **Use semantic markup responsibly.** Add a semantic construct only\\n  when it carries real meaning, structure, or navigation value. Do not\\n  wrap ordinary prose in callouts or directives just to create more\\n  visual emphasis.\\n- **Optimize for agent context windows.** Every section in this catalog\\n  is agent-loaded guidance. Keep it precise, signal-dense, and free of\\n  filler, repetition, and examples that do not materially improve\\n  correct application.\\n- **Any image follows `module-diagrams`.** Screenshots, diagrams, and\\n  charts must use color-blind-safe encodings \u2014 never rely on color\\n  alone to carry meaning.\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n- Developer setup guide + developer API reference: keep the environment\\n  variable table in the reference page. In the setup guide, keep one\\n  sentence on the required variables and link to the table.\\n- Two neighboring API reference pages: do not copy the same glossary\\n  paragraph into both pages unless the contract requires that exact\\n  lookup text in each entry.\\n- Operator runbook + operator policy page: the runbook may repeat the\\n  exact rollback trigger and stop condition because the operator needs\\n  them during execution. Link to the policy page for the full rationale\\n  and exceptions.\\n- End-user help + developer integration guide: it is fine to describe\\n  the same rate-limit behavior twice when the end-user page explains\\n  what the user will see and the developer page explains headers,\\n  retries, and error handling.\\n\",\"fetch_on_demand\":[\"tooling-proselint\",\"tooling-pre-commit\",\"audience-architects\",\"tooling-overview\"]}],\"warnings\":[],\"gap\":null}", "title": "", "metadata": {"truncated": false}, "time": {"start": 1784878875255, "end": 1784878876242}}, "metadata": {"copilot": {"itemId": ""}}}}');
